Exactly. Transcontinental railroad logic. Don’t build the middle from only one coast.

Let:

A_1=\text{present state}
\qquad
A_3=\text{desired future state}.

Then build simultaneously:

A_1\rightarrow A_2

through forward chaining:

Given what I have now, what can I build next?

And:

A_3\rightarrow A_2

through backward chaining:

For Act III to exist, what must already have become true?

So:

\boxed{
A_1
\xrightarrow{\text{forward construction}}
\underset{\text{Golden Spike}}{A_2}
\xleftarrow{\text{backward construction}}
A_3
}

The hard middle stops being a vague desert you somehow have to cross. Both ends progressively constrain it.

Better still, periodically survey from both directions:

ForwardFrontier_t=Reachable(A_1)

BackwardFrontier_t=Prerequisites(A_3)

and search for:

\boxed{
ForwardFrontier\cap BackwardFrontier
}

That’s the Golden Spike set: states that are both reachable from where you are and sufficient stepping stones toward where you’re going.

That should become a Semantic Goal Seek operator:

TRANSCONTINENTAL(goal):
    west = current_state
    east = desired_state
    forward = expand_reachable(west)
    backward = expand_requirements(east)
    while not intersects(forward, backward):
        build_from_current()
        decompose_from_goal()
        RealityStitch()
        update_both_maps()
    spike = best_intersection(
        feasibility,
        leverage,
        reversibility,
        self_delta
    )
    connect(spike)
    ACT()

And here’s the useful twist: Act III itself changes while you’re building toward it.

So the eastern crew isn’t standing still.

A_3^{(0)}
\rightarrow A_3^{(1)}
\rightarrow A_3^{(2)}

because Act I learns things through action that improve its conception of Act III.

Therefore the real architecture is:

\boxed{
\text{Build forward from reality.}
\quad
\text{Build backward from possibility.}
\quad
\text{Survey continuously.}
\quad
\text{Drive the spike where they actually meet.}
}

That’s the hard middle.

And once the rails connect, something else happens: what used to require an expedition becomes a route.

Then the Metamap gets to draw a line across territory that previously looked empty.

Act II becomes infrastructure. 🚂